so our brain continues to produce  dopamine all the time bleeping sounds of  our pc email coming in watching youtube  whatsapp the phone ringing our rooms are  basically like skinner boxes a bed a tv  a fridge nothing to do but to press the  lever for the drugs from analog back in  dopamine nation but sometimes our  addictions can go too far going crazy  watching tv for hours or binging on our  favorite foods that's what i want to  talk about a little bit this week doing  a dopamine detox so i was inspired after  the book by annalem beck on dopamine  nation and she talks about the dangers  of these dopamine inducing modern  appliances such as the internet but also  our easy availability to food and  different drugs and that this constant  dopamine rush is kind of disturbing our  natural ability to make dopamine in  normal situations because she states  when we are consuming high dopamine  rewards we lose the ability to take joy  in ordinary pleasures and after reading  the book of the dopamine nation i kind  of decided that i wanted to look at some  of my own habits and see  where i am going a little bit too far in  inducing dopamine so for example i have  the tendency when i'm really stressed to  binge watch a lot of netflix shows or to  read some of my favorite novels  throughout the evening and although i  don't think these are the worst habits i  do think i could be a little bit more  mindful of the bad habits that i have  and that's why after this book i have  conducted a list of five tips that if  you want to do a little dopamine detox  with me could probably help you reset  your normal dopamine homeostasis  so my first tip is to remove all the  distractions in your environment such to  make your environment as productive as  possible for your dopamine detox so for  example you can think of blocking  certain websites such as youtube or  netflix during certain hours of the day  or putting your phone on airplay mode or  removing it a little bit also if you  have the tendency to snack a lot on  sugary foods to remove these from your  environment such that you can go into  this dopamine detox as naturally as  possible the second tip i would give is  to also have a certain time slot in the  week where you try to remove all these  unnatural dopamine inducers as much as  possible so for example you can think of  the weekend and make you keep your  weekends media free or phone free and if  this is a little bit too much or you  find this a little bit too hard you can  also just pick a few hours of the day  for example in the evening or in the  mornings that you don't use any media at  all and just kind of see how this  affects you because in the book by anna  lambert the dopamine nation she does  talk about a little bit the anxiety we  usually feel when dopamine inducing  things or behaviors are removed from our  system so if you feel a little bit  anxious the first week just try to kind  of be mindful of that and just let it  sit or try to journal about it a little  bit because getting closer to our  emotions is usually one of the ways that  we can alleviate some of the bad habits  that we have formed the third tip i  would give is to get dopamine from  certain behaviors that are related to  pain so in the book she talks about  certain behaviors that are related to  pain or adversity that actually  increases dopamine naturally in our  system as well so one of the most well  known right now is this so-called cold  showers so wim hof discusses this  extensively in his book and he kind of  goes into the benefits of taking cold  showers but if you're really adverse to  cold showers like i am there are  different ways to get dopamine for more  pain inducing behaviors so one of the  other ones is exercise or intermittent  fasting and i think both of these are  pretty interesting because they kind of  put your body under a little bit of  stress and we know from research that a  little bit of stress can actually be  good for animals and also humans to  increase longevity so for example dr  uberman talks about this in his podcast  extensively of what the effects are of  intermittent fasting on the brain and  the body but basically it comes down to  hermesis and hormesis is this phenomenon  where adaptive responses of biological  systems to moderate environmental or  self-imposed challenges or stress  through which the system improves its  functionality and or tolerance to more  severe challenges or you can just say  there's no growth without pain this is  kind of the conclusion of that but i do  think this is a really interesting  mechanism and something you can  experiment with as well so the fourth  tip i would give is if you have some  habits that are a little bit bad to swap  them with better habits so for example  if you have the habit of snacking during  your studies to try to replace your  snacks with some healthier snacks or if  you have the habit of watching a lot of  tv to try to watch tv in another  language such that it may help your  language studies and i think by being a  little bit less strict on ourself and  just just gently swapping in some better  habits this is sometimes a little easier  than to totally remove all the bad  habits altogether and the last advice i  would give is to try to have in the year  a certain week or even a month that you  totally abstain from certain behaviors  that you deem negative because in the  book dopamine nation and liberte talks  about that if we want to reset the  reward pathway we have to abstain from  certain behaviors for about a month at  least  so i think this is in general really  hard but for example if you have a phone  addiction it would be good to see if you  have a period in a year where you could  remove your phone from your life for a  longer period of time and i think by  doing this you can really get deep down  into the root of what exactly is causing  some of the addictive behaviors you have  and i do think this is one of the  hardest challenges and that's why i  would recommend to do it for example in  the summer when we just have naturally a  little bit more free time so yeah these  were the tips i have for today but i  would highly recommend you to read the  book dopamine nation i think she talks  about a lot of interesting patients she  had and some of their addictions which  are really shocking but also sometimes  recognizable for myself as well so if  you have any tips and tricks for me i  would love to hear them so put them down  in the comments below and i also made a  video about motivation if you lack some  motivation in 2022 which i think is  really logical and otherwise see you  next week bye
 

